WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.440
Shut your eyes, cover your ears, and pinch
your nose, 'cause Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.220
is going to turn your brain to goo and send
it leaking out of your head.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.340
More specifically, the final shot of the upcoming
Star Wars flick will, quote, "melt your mind,"

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.760
according to diehard Star Wars fan and filmmaker
Kevin Smith.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.460
Speaking with IGN during 2019's San Diego
Comic-Con, Smith recalled his experience on

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.050
the set of The Rise of Skywalker, where director
J.J. Abrams and the film's crew were sending

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.780
him mixed signals about a certain set piece
that is, the set piece used for the closing

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.460
shot of the film, and thus the final frame
of the entire Skywalker saga.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:42.520
Smith detailed that some folks at Pinewood
Studios, where The Rise of Skywalker was being

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.019
filmed, advised him to not look at the final
shot set piece, as it's shocking enough to

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.260
anyone who sees it.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.640
On the other hand, some people urged Smith
to take a peek at it for that same reason.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:57.199
"There was a scuttlebutt about a set there
at Pinewood.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.219
'You have to
see this, when you see this it will melt your

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:01.720
mind.'"

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.540
When Smith asked why, Abrams told him it was
the last shot of the movie.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.100
"'You don't want to be spoiled.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.960
You want to be in a theater when this happens,
trust me.'

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.619
And then other people on the crew were like,
'Bro, I wish I hadn't seen it.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.740
I'm glad I did, but it will melt your mind.'"

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.340
Smith continued, noting that it was almost
impossible for him to resist the temptation

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.820
to look at the final shot of The Rise of Skywalker.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.080
Ultimately, though, his will power was strong
enough, and Smith walked away from his time

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.460
on the film's set with confidence that whatever
The Rise of Skywalker ends on, it will be

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.660
incredible thanks to JJ Abrams.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.400
He also underscored the importance of trusting
filmmakers and not cheating oneself out of

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.580
an amazing cinematic experience by looking
at spoilers.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.580
"But J.J.'s such a magical magician that I'm
just like, 'You know what?

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.420
Do it, trick me.'

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.600
I'll wait and get tricked.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.149
Even though I could've seen what it was, I
was like, 'I'll sit back.

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:50.820
I'll like your plan.'

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:52.140
He's never let me down so far.'"

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.940
What this final shot could be, we truly have
not a single clue.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.300
But whatever it is, someone told Smith that
it's pretty impressive.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.800
And that's...well, that's something, right?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.420
It's definitely not nothing.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.400
Okay, maybe it's only slightly better than
nothing.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:09.560
Because let's remember: when it comes to all
things Star Wars, Kevin Smith is not what

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.480
you might call an "objective source."

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.000
Remember his first movie, Clerks, which featured
an extended discussion on the ethics of blowing

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.360
up the second Death Star in Return of the
Jedi, which was likely filled with contractors

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.000
working hard to finish construction:

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.720
"Think the average Stormtrooper knows how
to install a toilet main?

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.800
All's they know is killing in white uniforms."

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.640
Think back to his second feature film, Mallrats,
a movie in which his character, Silent Bob,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.180
spent considerable screen time attempting
to master the Jedi Mind trick.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.220
"Knock it off!"

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.000
"The Force is strong with this one."

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.380
"Dude, don't encourage him."

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.720
And then Chasing Amy, his third movie, actually
features a long rant about the racial politics

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.260
of the original Star Wars trilogy in the film's
first batch of scenes.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.820
So while someone almost certainly told Smith
that watching that last scene would melt his

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.540
mind, it's important to remember some important
context: the crew-member was talking to Kevin

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:58.040
Smith.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.580
There's no doubt that watching the scene would've
melted his mind.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.220
"There are people in the world that know what
the last shot is, even if the movie's not

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.660
fully cut together at this point.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:07.800
I'm not one of them."

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:08.900
But as for the rest of us?

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.620
Well, let's just hope that the final scene
will leave our brains at least lightly singed.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.580
That's not too much to hope for, is it?

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.780
“AHHH!”

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.060
Prepare for a head-spinning experience when
Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker arrives in

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.560
theaters on December 20.

